You are working on the RML Platform.

Core stack rules:
- Use Laravel + Inertia React + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS.
- Use PostgreSQL.
- Use Laravel authentication/session auth.
- Use Spatie Laravel Permission for roles and permissions.
- Use policies for access control.
- Use services for business logic.
- Use form requests for backend validation.
- Use typed React props.
- Use environment variables for all secrets and external services.
- Do not hardcode API keys, payment keys, WhatsApp tokens, or private credentials.

UI/design rules:
- Use Untitled UI design principles and Untitled UI-style components as the default for all screens, layouts, forms, buttons, modals, cards, tables, dashboards, filters, sidebars, tabs, alerts, empty states, and responsive behaviours.
- Do not use generic shadcn-looking components unless they are heavily customised to match the RML / Untitled UI visual style.
- Match the provided Figma screenshots as closely as possible.
- Use the RML design system colours:
  - Primary Green: #16a34a
  - Primary Light: #dcfce7
  - Primary Lighter: #f0fdf4
  - Sidebar Navy: #0f172a
  - Blue: #2563eb
  - Blue Light: #eff6ff
  - Amber: #d97706
  - Red: #dc2626
  - Text: #111827
  - Muted: #6b7280
  - Border: #e5e7eb
  - Background: #f8fafc
  - Card: #ffffff
- Use Inter as the main font.
- Use a mono font for IDs such as LD-1041, PAY-00091, TXN-9921.
- Use Logo 2 — RML Energy Exchange.
- Keep the design premium, clean, professional, and suitable for a B2B SaaS marketplace / CRM / operations dashboard.
- Use light mode only for the MVP.
- Logged-in portals must use a dark navy sidebar, white content cards, green CTAs, status badges, clean data tables, and polished dashboard layouts.
- Public website must be one scrollable landing page, not separate Buy/Sell/Contact pages.
- Remove the Figma prototype top navigation from the real app. Only build real public/app navigation.

Responsive and media query rules:
- Everything must be responsive from the start.
- Do not build desktop-only screens.
- Every page, component, form, table, modal, dashboard, and portal must work properly on mobile, tablet, laptop, and desktop.
- Use mobile-first Tailwind responsive utilities for visual layout whenever possible.
- Use React media queries efficiently for conditional rendering, heavy layout changes, table-to-card switching, sidebar behaviour, modal/drawer switching, and performance-sensitive responsive logic.
- Create a reusable typed `useMediaQuery` hook using `window.matchMedia`.
- The `useMediaQuery` hook must:
  - be SSR-safe
  - clean up listeners properly
  - avoid unnecessary re-renders
  - not use inefficient continuous resize listeners
  - support common breakpoints such as mobile, tablet, desktop, and large desktop
- Prefer CSS/Tailwind breakpoints for simple styling changes.
- Prefer React media queries only when the rendered structure should actually change.
- Do not render large desktop tables on mobile if a mobile card layout is more suitable.
- Do not hide large duplicated desktop/mobile layouts unless necessary; avoid rendering unnecessary heavy components.
- On mobile:
  - collapse the sidebar into a drawer or mobile navigation
  - convert wide tables into stacked cards where needed
  - make forms single-column
  - make modals usable on small screens
  - use drawers/bottom sheets where better than desktop modals
  - make file upload components mobile-friendly
  - make filters wrap neatly or collapse into a filter drawer
  - make buyer payment summary bars sticky at the bottom where useful
  - avoid horizontal overflow completely
  - ensure buttons are easy to tap
  - ensure text remains readable
- On tablet:
  - avoid cramped multi-column layouts
  - use 2-column grids only where space allows
  - keep tables readable or switch to card/table hybrid layouts
- On desktop:
  - match the Figma dashboard/card/table layouts closely
  - keep the dark sidebar fixed
  - use full data tables where appropriate
- Always test responsive behaviour before marking a page complete.

Suggested media query hook pattern:
- Create `resources/js/hooks/use-media-query.ts`.
- Use this hook across responsive components instead of repeating matchMedia logic.
- Example usage:
  - `const isMobile = useMediaQuery('(max-width: 767px)')`
  - `const isTablet = useMediaQuery('(min-width: 768px) and (max-width: 1023px)')`
  - `const isDesktop = useMediaQuery('(min-width: 1024px)')`
- Use it for:
  - Sidebar desktop vs mobile drawer
  - DataTable desktop view vs mobile card list
  - Audit modal desktop modal vs mobile drawer
  - Filter bar desktop inline filters vs mobile filter drawer
  - Payment summary desktop inline card vs mobile sticky bottom bar

Business/product rules:
- MVP only: no subscription billing.
- No homeowner dashboard in MVP.
- Homeowner free installation enquiry is public form only.
- Do not expose public pricing.
- Do not expose customer details before payment.
- Do not expose buyer details to sellers.
- Do not expose seller details to buyers.
- Sellers must not see internal pricing, margins, or buyer information.
- Buyers must only see zone, distance, size, scheme, unit price, and total price before payment.
- Each lead can only be sold to one buyer.
- Commissions are percentage-based and become due only when the lead is sold and buyer payment is confirmed.
- Account approval is required for sellers and buyers before dashboard access.
- Internal auditor/staff portal must exist in MVP.
- EN / ES / FR language switcher must be functional in MVP.

Code quality rules:
- Keep code modular and production-minded.
- Reuse components instead of duplicating UI.
- Create reusable RML/Untitled UI-style components for:
  - AppLayout
  - PublicLayout
  - AuthLayout
  - PortalSidebar
  - PortalHeader
  - MobileSidebarDrawer
  - KPI cards
  - DataTable
  - MobileCardList
  - StatusBadge
  - FilterBar
  - MobileFilterDrawer
  - Alert
  - Modal
  - Drawer
  - FormInput
  - Select
  - Textarea
  - FileUpload
  - Tabs
  - EmptyState
  - Pagination
  - PaymentSummaryBar
  - AuditChecklist
- Keep React components typed and clean.
- Keep backend logic out of React components where possible.
- Use Laravel services for pricing, payments, commissions, distance calculation, lead release, and audit logging.
- Use policies and permissions for all role-based access.
- Create audit logs for key actions.
- Use migrations, seeders, factories, policies, requests, controllers, services, and tests where appropriate.
- Run tests/formatting after major changes.
- Do not overwrite important existing code without checking.
- NEVER wipe the local Postgres DB (`migrate:fresh`, `migrate:refresh`, `db:wipe`). Tests use sqlite memory. If demo users missing, run `db:seed` only. Demo login: admin@rml.test / password.